rial Department is seeking an Individualized Study Tutor for the following course: HSRV 311: Social Justice in Action - Link to Syllabus Qualifications and Experience Required: A minimum of a Master's degree required (PhD preferred) in social work, psychology, counselling psychology, or a closely related field. Professional registration or designation as a Registered Social Worker (RSW), Registered Psychologist (R.Psych), or equivalent is strongly preferred. The successful candidate will have demonstrated knowledge and applied experience in decolonial, anti-oppressive, trauma-informed, and socially just approaches to human services practice. Experience working with Indigenous Ways of Knowing, Etuaptmumk (Two-Eyed Seeing), and culturally responsive approaches to care is essential. The tutor will demonstrate an understanding of the historical and ongoing impacts of colonization, systemic oppression, and inequities, alongside a commitment to ethical, relational, and community-centred practices. They will be responsible for providing academic assistance to students, for marking assignments, and for assigning and recording final grades.  Teaching experience at a post-secondary level is required. Experience in distance learning and familiarity with digital teaching technologies and online course delivery is preferred.  Experience with adult education and Brightspace online course delivery is considered an asset. Along with the relevant academic credentials as mentioned above, this position requires excellent communication, organizational, administration and online/computer skills.  The successful candidate should have demonstrated ability to maintain a high academic standard, maintain accurate student records, and return students' work promptly and within the University's required service standards.  Candidates must own or have access to a computer and internet with the capability to communicate with the University and students and the capability to run any applications r